  "chunk_content": "Output  ```bash {\"id\":\"chatcmpl-6v7mkQj980V1yBec6ETrKPRqFjNw9\", \"object\":\"chat.completion\",\"created\":1679072642, \"model\":\"gpt-35-turbo\", \"usage\":{\"prompt_tokens\":58, \"completion_tokens\":68, \"total_tokens\":126}, \"choices\":[{\"message\":{\"role\":\"assistant\", \"content\":\"Yes, other Azure AI services also support customer managed keys. Azure AI services offer multiple options for customers to manage keys, such as using Azure Key Vault, customer-managed keys in Azure Key Vault or customer-managed keys through Azure Storage service. This helps customers ensure that their data is secure and access to their services is controlled.\"},\"finish_reason\":\"stop\",\"index\":0}]} ```  Output formatting adjusted for ease of reading, actual output is a single block of text without line breaks.  > [!div class=\"nextstepaction\"] > I ran into an issue when running the code sample.  ### Understanding the message structure  The GPT-35-Turbo and GPT-4 models are optimized to work with inputs formatted as a conversation.  The `messages` variable passes an array of dictionaries with different roles in the conversation delineated by system, user, and assistant. The system message can be used to prime the model by including context or instructions on how the model should respond.  The GPT-35-Turbo & GPT-4 how-to guide provides an in-depth introduction into the options for communicating with these new models.",
